{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017gZ8j4e59M2KbYDTTbkQVc","type":"tool_result","content":"ideas:\n01_empirical_bayes_latent_difficulty_posterior_interval\n02_parametric_bootstrap_pivot_calibration_of_eb_irt_interval\n03_eb_irt_with_config_by_subject_interaction_random_effects\n\ntask:\ncapture.py\nevaluate.py\nprogram_0\nrequirements.txt\ntask.md\n{\n  \"hypothesis\": \"The interval score at level 0.90 is minimised by the 5% and 95% quantiles of the predictive distribution of the selected config's fresh-question accuracy y. The naive reporter (best search score + Wilson) is biased upward (winner's curse) and too narrow in the 'many configs, few questions' regime. Searcher-agnostic debiasing should lower the mean interval score: fit a hierarchical logistic latent-difficulty model P(correct)=sigmoid(a_k - b_q), with a_k ~ N(0,tau^2) and b_q ~ N(mu_subject, s^2), to ALL logged (question, config, outcome) triples with tau and s set by empirical Bayes. Because both exhaustive search and successive halving choose configs using only already-observed outcomes, selection is ignorable for the likelihood. The posterior of theta_k = E_b[sigmoid(a_k - b)] for the selected config is therefore valid under either searcher without a searcher-specific correction. Shrinkage toward the pooled mean removes the winner's-curse bias; with tau estimated near 0 it handles the all-configs-equal worlds. Posterior quantiles (Laplace draws of a_k, mu, s, then Gauss-Hermite integration over fresh difficulties) give the interval. Expected result: lower mean interval score than the naive Wilson baseline (combined_score -0.232), with the biggest gain at large K, small N and the halving searcher. Failure mode: real MMLU-Pro outcomes are multi-dimensional (subject x config interactions), so the 1-D model can be miscalibrated there. A held-out-question calibration widening factor and a bootstrap-style fallback are the safeguards.\",\n  \"idea_id\": \"c01_eb_irt_posterior_interval\",\n  \"implementation_plan\": [\n    \"In program_1/src/program.py, keep the handle(request) python_rpc_v1 interface. reset stores the seed and returns exactly {'ok': True}. report returns exactly estimate, lower and upper, clipped so that 0<=lower<=estimate<=upper<=1 and all values are finite floats.\",\n    \"Decode log.question_idx, log.config_idx, log.outcome and subject_code from base64 little-endian arrays with np.frombuffer (dtypes uint16/uint8/uint8/uint8). Work only with arrays from the request, using no files or network.\",\n    \"Implement _fit_irt(q, c, y, subj, N, K): MAP fit of logit P = a[c] - b[q] by alternating damped Newton steps. Ridge priors a ~ N(0,tau^2), b ~ N(mu_subject, s^2), with a per-subject mean shrunk to the global mean. Update tau^2 and s^2 each outer iteration from the posterior second moments. Include the Laplace/trace correction so tau^2 does not collapse or inflate. Clamp tau^2 to [1e-4, 4].\",\n    \"Work only with sparse triples so the same code handles the exhaustive (N*K entries) and halving (sparse log) searchers. Use vectorised numpy with np.bincount for the per-config and per-question gradient and Hessian diagonals. Cap total outer iterations (about 30) and add a wall-clock guard (about 1.5 s) that returns the best current fit.\",\n    \"Posterior for the selected config: approximate Var(a_k) by the inverse of the diagonal Hessian plus the uncertainty in mu. Draw S (about 400) samples of (a_k, mu_global, log s) with the reset-seeded np.random.default_rng. For each draw compute theta = sum_j w_j * sigmoid(a_k - mu_sub_mix - s*x_j) using np.polynomial.hermite_e.hermegauss nodes, mixing over the empirical subject distribution when subject_code is not all zero.\",\n    \"Report estimate = posterior median or mean of theta, lower = np.quantile(theta, 0.05) and upper = np.quantile(theta, 0.95). Then apply a width-calibration factor kappa >= 1, applied around the estimate, that depends on min(N, evidence), K and whether subject_code is all zero (real vs synthetic).\",\n    \"Degenerate guards. If all outcomes are 0, return estimate 0, lower 0 and an upper bound no greater than a rule-of-three bound (about 3/n_selected_obs, capped, e.g. <=0.02). If the selected config has no observations, fall back to the pooled mean with a wide interval. If tau is estimated at its lower clamp (all configs equal), report a pooled-mean interval.\",\n    \"Build a local dev harness OUTSIDE program_1/src (for example dev/sim.py). It should generate synthetic latent-difficulty worlds across N in {50..2000} and K in {2..64}, plus equal-config worlds, and run both exhaustive and successive-halving searchers. It should also replay ../../task/resources/search/outcomes.npz (24 configs) with both searchers on subsampled question sets, compute the true y from held-out questions, and compute the mean interval score IS=(u-l)+20*max(l-y,0)+20*max(y-u,0). Compare the naive Wilson baseline, the posterior interval without calibration, and the calibrated version.\",\n    \"Use the dev harness to choose kappa and the clamps on the search split only. Check coverage near 0.90 in each (searcher, K, N) stratum. Check that the average per-call time is <=1 s and the worst-case is <=5 s on 4 threads. Set OMP/MKL threads to <=4. Optionally add a held-out-question calibration check inside report (a fit on 80% of questions, scoring the selected config on the ocapture.py\nevaluate.py\nevaluate_final.py\nprogram_0\nrequirements.txt\nresources\ntask.md\ntask_contract.json\n# Goal\n\n**研究任务：自动调优之后，报告的基准分数意味着什么**\n**背景与动机**\n在大语言模型（LLM）的实际使用中，研究者常常在一个基准的题目上自动搜索提示词（prompt）\n或由多个 LLM 调用组成的程序（pipeline），再把搜索得到的\"最佳配置\"在同一批题目上的得分\n作为该系统的性能汇报。所用的自动搜索工具多种多样。\n\n这种做法引出一个统计问题：基准题目既被用来**挑选**配置，又被用来给该配置**打分**，因此\n汇报的数字不一定反映系统在全新题目上的真实表现；基于这类汇报数字做出的比较和决定是否\n可靠，也就不清楚。\n\n**研究问题**\n请围绕下面的问题开展一项完整的研究：\n\n1. 对一个先在有限题集上搜索配置、再把选出的配置拿去使用的系统，汇报的分数与它在全新\n   题目上的真实表现相差多少？差距受哪些因素影响？它是否会影响基于汇报分数做出的结论？\n2. 能否设计更可靠的汇报方式？应如何通过实验检验它是否可靠？\n\n**成功如何衡量**\n结论需要有你自己运行的实验支持，并至少在一个真实 LLM 与真实基准的设置上检验；实验设计、\n统计工具和理论分析的深度由你决定。结论可以是支持、反驳或视条件而定。\n\n**关键约束**\n资源约束：一周墙钟时间；以 CPU 为主，涉及 LLM 推理的实验可使用至多一张 24 GB GPU 或等价\nAPI 预算（详见 `resources.md`）；可用数据见 `data.md`；最终产出是一篇研究论文及复现代码\n（详见 `deliverable.md`）。\n\n**起步文献**\n以下条目均为 2025 年及以前公开的工作，仅作背景知识参考，不要求全部使用，也欢迎自行检索其他早期文献。\n\n1. Wang et al. *MMLU-Pro: A More Robust and Challenging Multi-Task Language Understanding\n   Benchmark*. NeurIPS 2024 Datasets & Benchmarks（arXiv 2024-06-03）。\n   https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.01574\n   说明：提出一个多学科、每题 10 个选项的多项选择语言理解基准，在 MMLU 的基础上增加了推理型题目，并评测了多种 LLM。\n2. Khattab et al. *DSPy: Compiling Declarative Language Model Calls into Self-Improving\n   Pipelines*. arXiv 2023-10-05。https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.03714\n   说明：提出以声明式模块编写 LLM 调用程序的编程模型，并提供按指定评价指标自动优化其提示词和示范样例\nShell cwd was reset to /workspace/paper","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f59c0e66-4bc0-42fb-84a9-3fc6dfe47561","uuid":"e7c853c5-5096-4dcc-9b47-4cccc42585eb","timestamp":"2026-11-11T14:27:08.371Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"ideas:\n01_empirical_bayes_latent_difficulty_posterior_interval\n02_parametric_bootstrap_pivot_calibration_of_eb_irt_interval\n03_eb_irt_with_config_by_subject_interaction_random_effects\n\ntask:\ncapture.py\nevaluate.py\nprogram_0\nrequirements.txt\ntask.md\n{\n  \"hypothesis\": \"The interval score at level 0.90 is minimised by the 5% and 95% quantiles of the predictive distribution of the selected config's fresh-question accuracy y. The naive reporter (best search score + Wilson) is biased upward (winner's curse) and too narrow in the 'many configs, few questions' regime. Searcher-agnostic debiasing should lower the mean interval score: fit a hierarchical logistic latent-difficulty model P(correct)=sigmoid(a_k - b_q), with a_k ~ N(0,tau^2) and b_q ~ N(mu_subject, s^2), to ALL logged (question, config, outcome) triples with tau and s set by empirical Bayes. Because both exhaustive search and successive halving choose configs using only already-obs